+
+### Spell-shop presentation follow-up
+
+In enabled spell-shop matches, migrated spell descriptions use cast/target wording in all five shipped languages, retaining the original values and rich-text tags. Equipment and native Arts descriptions are unchanged. Migrated spells and native Arts share a purple frame with a second border/diamond marker on the prep bench, including the simplified board, temporary slots and WebGL item plates. Disabled matches retain the original presentation. Regression checks: `test/ui/spell-presentation.test.js`, `test/render/spell-frame.test.js` and `test/ui/spell-shop.e2e.test.js`.
+
+### Optional spell catalog UI
+
+The independent spell slot and owned spell frames use the shared catalog. Card text shows both choose-one effects; after casting, the existing shop reward area presents exactly two choices. Shop operator cards show the effective purchase-layer bonus and an elite badge where applicable. Life-priced spells show their life cost. Support uses an empty ground-tile drag target and a read-only pending operator preview. Locked discovered operators show a lock and cannot be moved or used as spell targets. Runtime art is separate from the patch.
+
+Spell and equipment cards stay before the funds column at narrow widths; only the operator/reward strip scrolls. An all-spell refresh has its own inner scroll strip, keeping its category label visible. Purchase bonuses use a high-contrast purple card badge and a tooltip naming the affected Bonds. Browser previews use an actual post-cast private shop view, label themselves as mock scenes and use base prices rather than the mock fixture's randomized markup examples.
+
+Spell illustration fill: shop, detail, fallback bench and WebGL plates use centered cover cropping only for downloaded spell illustrations. Migrated native item icons retain contain sizing without zoom or clipping, including identity tags; the purple spell frame still applies. Downloaded Hearthstone spell art has uniform exported matte edges removed by the asset tool and uses cover within clipped bounds. Native preview icons use contain with explicit size bounds.
+
+Touch of Gold: marked shop slots grant one native promotion reward on successful purchase only; failed/repeated purchases and devour do not grant it. This uses the reward queue without firing a synthetic merge event. Shop cards show gold borders, gold tier chips and an explicit purchase-reward label. Purchase-layer bonuses occupy a dedicated top header above the portrait and price row.
+
+Targeted refresh / Support refinement: Wine fills current-level shop capacity (operator slots + equipment slots + one spell slot). Hamuul fills only current-level operator positions and preserves equipment and spell positions, including sold/frozen state. All-spell shops use the full merchandise row, with scrolling only at narrow widths. Reserved Support units can be reoriented with g.move on their own tile during editable prep; another player, a different destination and non-prep requests are refused. Private support records and prep previews carry dir; battle input uses it. Support always receives innate taunt at battle start. The right-side Support controls expose four directions and the taunt label.
+
+Spell refinements (user design, 2026-10-15): Headhunting uses trait eventType SERVER_GAIN, not an effectKey prefix. Texas (sale) and Catherine (prep start) are excluded; all visible shop operators and 120 discovery rolls across levels 1-6 are audited. Support, Nozdormu and Sharing each allow one successful cast per round. Failed casts do not spend the allowance; refused extra copies remain held and become usable next round. Private shop spellUsed drives disabled cast buttons and drag feedback. Shop spell icons omit the redundant text tag, retaining the category heading and frame.
+
